00:08:01.320 --> 00:08:07.620
retail investors get wrong when it comes to their TFSA, and it can quietly erode returns over time.

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:13.580
Imagine you bought shares of a US dividend paying stock in your TFSA at the start of the year when

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:20.280
interest rates were higher, with your order filling at $45 per share. The 15% US withholding tax on

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:27.840
dividends means you only keep 85% of the payout reducing your effective yield from 3% to 2.55%,

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:34.340
and there's no way to claim it back in a TFSA. The pro tip is that professionals always check the

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:40.680
foreign withholding tax rate and prefer growth stocks over high dividend ones in TFS, as to avoid

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:46.420
this drag on returns. The biggest mistake with TFSA investing is treating it like a high interest savings

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:52.160
account instead of a powerful tax-free growth vehicle. Instead, always prioritize high growth assets

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:57.740
like tech or emerging market ETFs within your contribution room to maximize compounding over decades.

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:02.980
Track your contribution room annually through the CRA's My Account to avoid the 1% monthly

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:08.240
over contribution penalty and be cautious with frequent trading as the CRA may scrutinize day trading

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:14.860
in registered accounts. For a moderate risk tolerance, a model approach is 50% in broad market ETFs,

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:21.800
30% in individual growth stocks, 15% in bonds, or fixed income, and 5% in cash for opportunities.

